      "asked": "2026-11-17",
      "sent": "Answer to \"Server actions: calling, before, after and failure\" (/Users/owen/www/spell/spell.epics/epics/data/details/server-actions.html), sent 2026-11-18T01:03:16.201Z:\n  15 of 15 decided\n  Q1 · Calling by id:  complete job 7151:  A · complete job 7151, yours (recommended)\n  Q2 · A job already held:  A · Use the held copy (recommended)\n  Q3 · More than one parameter:  A · Spell's own action forms (recommended)\n  Q4 · Listing the parameters under the action:  Other:  `to \"complete a job\"` or `to complete a job` so it matches the normal action definition\n  Q5 · Showing the action on its type:  A · Shown by Spell, no line (recommended)\n  Q6 · The words for before and after:  A · before / after completing (recommended)\n  Q7 · The words for a failure step:  A · if completing a job fails (recommended)\n  Q8 · The wrapping action, with call the server:  A · Both ways, yours kept (recommended)\n  Q9 · How the levels stack:  B · Three levels\n  Q10 · A page's steps when the page isn't shown:  A · Only while it's shown (recommended)\n  Q11 · The kinds of error a step can name:  B · Your words added\n  Q12 · What catching an error means:  A · A branch that matched (recommended);  Other:  `pass the error` like we can pass an event.\n\nThe after `show the login dialog` one is tricky, \"nevermind\" after that doesn't read right.  Maybe `show the login dialog` and then `return` or `cancel` or `stop` as alternatives?\n  Q13 · nevermind, and how it differs from cancel:  A · nevermind, yours (recommended);  Other:  `do nothing` is good, bud I like `nevermind` as well\n  Q14 · Passing on, beside if that fails:  Other:  `if that fails` is like `if the error is anything` so it swallows any type of error.\n  Q15 · The notice nobody's steps caught:  A · Held when caught (recommended)\n  Comments:\n    Q10 · A page's steps when the page isn't shown, A · Only while it's shown:  hmm, the page after should take effect even if we navigate\n    Q15 · The notice nobody's steps caught, A · Held when caught:  This should be something tunable at the app level, e.g. so I can say/do something different, or have the auth logic defined once.\n\n`when any server call fails\n    if the error is \"not authorized\"\n       show the login page\n       return\n`\nSo app logic can skip the auth check.\n\nIf I don't want the default error notice to show up, I can put an `otherwise do nothing` in there\n    Q2 · A job already held, A · Use the held copy:  if they want to make sure they always have a fresh copy, they can load it explicitly\n    Q7 · The words for a failure step, A · if completing a job fails:  both `if completing...` and `when completing...`\n    Q8 · The wrapping action, with call the server, A · Both ways, yours kept:  In the netbox api page, `the server` should be an alias for `netbox`\ne.g. `netbox is a web server` => implies that the base class for netbox is `web server` whose base class is `server`.\n\n`call netbox` should work as well\n\nNot sure if files (CSV etc) should have special syntax for now, let's assume they are \"call the server\" for now.\n    Q9 · How the levels stack, B · Three levels:  A page is part of an app.\nI'd be OK defining page/app files as `foo.page.spell` and `foo.app.spell` if it makes things easier\n  Bullhorn comments, sent with it (answer each:  epic comments answer epics/data/details/server-actions.html#<id> --file <reply.html>):\n    C1 on \"can call by id\":  call by id as well as by loaded object\n    C2 on \"when the button is pressed\":  The below brings up the possibility of \n`while a job is being completed...`\nfor transient things like the page hint\n    C3 on \"\"no such place\"\":  server not found\n    C4 on \"ceiling\":  `limit` ?\n    C5 on \"not authorized\":  401 and 403 should be specific errors, e.g. \n- if \"not authorized\", you may want to let them log in.  - If \"not permitted\", you may want to let them save locally\n\nReading http status codes is for chumps.",
